# NON-FUNCTIONAL STRAWMAN v2-b (imagination-tier; conventions per a-fire-morning's
# final human layer). The HEALTHY-GUARD case at realistic width: a wide book, TWO
# undescribed walls, a value-flow participant, and a question about a line that is
# fine -- nothing went wrong; the user is just curious why it keeps not-skipping.
# Exercises: presence-complete participating-lines at scale (selection shows how
# MUCH each participant gets, never whether) -- multi-wall join with prose
# restatement -- guard anatomy as as-shipped sh -- the no-suspect NEXT STEPS shape
# (improvements, not repairs) -- value-flow provenance (PORT) -- pure ASCII.

-- web.sh (context, abbreviated; 24 lines, 9 tool-sites) --
 3  PORT=443
 5  apt-get update
 6  dpkg -s nginx >/dev/null 2>&1 || apt-get install -y nginx
 7  cp ./nginx.conf /etc/nginx/nginx.conf
 9  certsync push web1
11  systemctl enable --now nginx
18  corp-agent enroll --renew
21  hork tune --profile web
23  ufw allow "$PORT"/tcp

-- world (context) --
Steady-state morning; everything converged. No risk flag. corp-agent and hork are
both undescribed, so line 23 guards on every apply, forever; the admin finally
asks why it never just goes away.

-- replay --
$ dorc why web.sh:23
   [ web.sh, participating lines:
 3 | PORT=443
18 | corp-agent enroll --renew
21 | hork tune --profile web
23 | ufw allow "$PORT"/tcp

   === OUTCOME (2026-07-25 09:14:02) ===

   23|ufw guarded RATHER THAN skipped: two undescribed commands really ran
   above it. A fact reported before they ran cannot be trusted after; the
   guard re-asks the question live, in place, where nothing can go stale.

   === ANALYSIS ===

   what was known, and why it was not enough:

   * ufw.oracle.sh:44   reported  "Firewall:443/tcp@allowed"
                                  (ran 09:13:51, rc 0; 443 resolved from 3|PORT)
   * ufw.oracle.sh:41   vouches   "re-allowing an already-@allowed rule adds
                                  nothing worth running"
   ! 18|corp-agent                ran at 09:13:58 -- undescribed: no author has
                                  said what it touches, so it may touch anything
   ! 21|hork                      ran at 09:14:00 -- undescribed, same standing

   so: the report was good when it ran, but 18|corp-agent and 21|hork each cast
   a wall between the report and 23|ufw's turn. What dorc shipped instead of a
   skip -- your line, untouched, behind the oracle's own live check:

      ( ufw__is_converged allow "$PORT"/tcp ) \
         || ufw allow "$PORT"/tcp

   at apply, the check answered rc 0 in 40ms; the original never ran. The book
   stays fast and safe -- but not shorter: this line returns on every apply
   until the walls above it are described.

   === NEXT STEPS ===

   nothing is wrong. to make this line skippable:

      describe: hork and corp-agent are the walls. a convergence check for
                either (~2 lines of sh) removes its wall whenever it is
                converged; describing BOTH lets 23|ufw skip on proof
                (dorc teach walls)

      review:   `dorc why web.sh:23 --all` (every link, unselected, exhaustive)

(Receipt analysis: the run's own records, nothing re-measured. Filtered for
presumed relevance; `--all` enumerates everything the engine holds.)
